::ALG2_U04_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>What model represents 1200 dollars earning 5% annually? {
= A(t)=1200(1.05)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The principal is 1200 and the annual growth factor is 1.05.
~ A(t)=1200(0.95)<sup>t</sup>#This models 5% decrease.
~ A(t)=1.05(1200)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses principal and factor.
~ A(t)=1200+5t#This is linear growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>What factor represents 8% annual interest? {
~ 0.08#This is only the rate.
= 1.08#Correct. Interest growth uses 1 + 0.08 = 1.08.
~ 0.92#This models 8% depreciation.
~ 1.80#This models 80% growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>What factor represents 12% depreciation? {
~ 1.12#This models appreciation.
~ 0.12#This is the rate lost, not the remaining value.
= 0.88#Correct. If 12% is lost, 88% remains.
~ 1.88#This does not represent 12% depreciation.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>In V(t)=18000(0.85)<sup>t</sup>, what does 0.85 mean? {
~ 85% is lost each year.#If 85% were lost, the factor would be 0.15.
~ The starting value is 0.85.#The starting value is 18000.
~ The value grows by 85%.#A factor below 1 indicates decay, not growth.
= 85% of the value remains each year.#Correct. A factor of 0.85 means the value keeps 85% each period.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>In A(t)=500(1.03)<sup>t</sup>, what is the annual rate? {
= 3% growth#Correct. The factor 1.03 means 1 + 0.03, or 3% growth.
~ 103% growth#The new amount is 103% of the previous amount, which is 3% growth.
~ 3% decay#The factor is greater than 1, so it is growth.
~ 0.03% growth#0.03 as a decimal equals 3%.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A computer worth 1600 loses 25% each year. Which model fits? {
~ V(t)=1600(1.25)<sup>t</sup>#This models appreciation.
= V(t)=1600(0.75)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. If 25% is lost, 75% remains.
~ V(t)=0.75(1600)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the parameters.
~ V(t)=1600-25t#This subtracts 25 dollars each year.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A savings account starts at 700 and grows by 2% annually. Which model fits? {
~ A(t)=700(0.98)<sup>t</sup>#This models 2% decay.
~ A(t)=1.02(700)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the parameters.
= A(t)=700(1.02)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The principal is 700 and the growth factor is 1.02.
~ A(t)=700+2t#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes compound interest? {
~ Interest is added only once.#That does not describe compounding.
~ The balance decreases every year.#Compound interest usually models growth unless other payments change the balance.
~ The rate is ignored.#The rate determines the growth factor.
= Interest earns additional interest over time.#Correct. Compound interest applies growth to the updated balance each period.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A model B(t)=2500(1.04)<sup>t</sup> is evaluated at t=3. What does the result represent? {
= Balance after 3 time periods#Correct. The input t=3 gives the amount after three compounding intervals.
~ The interest rate#The rate is represented by the factor 1.04.
~ The initial balance#The initial balance is 2500.
~ The monthly payment#No payment is represented in this model.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which model shows 6% annual depreciation from 30000 dollars? {
~ V(t)=30000(1.06)<sup>t</sup>#This models appreciation.
= V(t)=30000(0.94)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Depreciation by 6% means 94% remains.
~ V(t)=6(30000)<sup>t</sup>#This uses the rate incorrectly.
~ V(t)=30000-6t#This is a fixed dollar decrease.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which model shows 6% annual appreciation from 30000 dollars? {
~ V(t)=30000(0.94)<sup>t</sup>#This models depreciation.
~ V(t)=6(30000)<sup>t</sup>#This uses the rate incorrectly.
= V(t)=30000(1.06)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Appreciation by 6% means a factor of 1.06.
~ V(t)=30000+6t#This adds 6 dollars each year.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does principal mean in a financial model? {
~ The annual rate#The rate is the percent change.
~ The exponent#The exponent represents time periods.
~ The final amount only#The final amount depends on time and rate.
= The starting amount#Correct. Principal is the original amount invested, borrowed, or valued.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor shows that an asset keeps 78% of its value each year? {
= 0.78#Correct. Keeping 78% means multiplying by 0.78 each year.
~ 1.78#This indicates growth.
~ 0.22#This would mean only 22% remains.
~ 78#This is not decimal factor form.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A 4000 dollar balance grows by factor 1.05 for 2 years. Which expression evaluates the balance? {
~ 4000(2)<sup>1.05</sup>#This reverses time and factor.
= 4000(1.05)<sup>2</sup>#Correct. Use principal times the factor raised to the number of years.
~ 1.05(4000)<sup>2</sup>#This reverses principal and factor.
~ 4000+1.05(2)#This is not compound growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A 9000 dollar asset is modeled by V(t)=9000(0.70)<sup>t</sup>. What is the depreciation rate? {
~ 70%#This is the percent remaining.
~ 0.70%#This confuses the factor with a percent rate.
= 30%#Correct. If 70% remains, 30% is lost each period.
~ 130%#This does not describe depreciation from factor 0.70.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which is a fair way to compare two investment models? {
~ Compare one after 2 years and one after 8 years.#Different time spans are not directly comparable.
~ Ignore principal because only rates matter.#Starting amount matters.
~ Ignore rates because only principal matters.#Rates affect growth over time.
= Compare both balances after the same number of years.#Correct. Using the same time value supports a fair comparison.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>If A(t)=1000(1.06)<sup>t</sup>, what is A(1)? {
= 1060#Correct. After one year, multiply 1000 by 1.06.
~ 1006#This adds 6 instead of 6%.
~ 1600#This treats 6% as 60%.
~ 6000#This multiplies by 6 instead of 1.06.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>If V(t)=20000(0.9)<sup>t</sup>, what is V(2)? {
~ 18000#This is V(1).
= 16200#Correct. Compute 20000 times 0.9 squared.
~ 16000#This subtracts 10% twice from the original instead of compounding.
~ 2000#This uses only 10% of the starting value.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor would produce the fastest growth? {
~ 1.02#This grows, but more slowly than 1.12.
~ 0.98#This is decay.
= 1.12#Correct. Among these, 1.12 is the largest growth factor.
~ 0.88#This is faster decay, not growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor would produce the fastest depreciation? {
~ 0.95#This depreciates slowly.
~ 1.03#This is growth.
~ 1.20#This is growth.
= 0.70#Correct. The smallest positive factor loses the greatest percent each period.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A loan balance grows by 1% monthly if unpaid. Which factor applies each month? {
= 1.01#Correct. A 1% monthly increase has factor 1.01 per month.
~ 0.99#This models 1% decrease.
~ 0.01#This is only the rate.
~ 1.10#This represents 10% growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which context is depreciation? {
~ A savings account earns 3% interest.#That is growth.
= A phone loses 20% of its resale value each year.#Correct. Losing value over time is depreciation.
~ A stock gains 4% in a month.#That is appreciation or growth.
~ A scholarship fund grows by donations.#That is growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which context is compound growth? {
~ A person saves exactly 50 dollars each month.#That is linear saving.
~ A bill is reduced by a fixed 10 dollars.#That is linear decrease.
= An account earns 5% interest on the updated balance each year.#Correct. Growth on the updated balance is compound growth.
~ A fee is charged once.#A one-time fee is not repeated compound growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>For A(t)=P(1.04)<sup>t</sup>, what does P represent? {
~ Percent rate#The percent rate is 4%.
~ Time#The variable t is time.
~ Growth factor#The growth factor is 1.04.
= Principal#Correct. P is the starting amount in the model.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A 500 dollar investment and an 800 dollar investment both earn 5%. Which value differs in their models? {
= Principal#Correct. The starting amount is different while the growth factor is the same.
~ Growth factor#Both use factor 1.05.
~ Annual rate#Both earn 5%.
~ Exponent variable#Both can use the same time variable.
}

